package server

const mcpSimulationPolicy = "Process Simulator is a first-class diagram type process-simulator with a separate simulation payload type:process-simulator,schemaVersion:1. GET /simulation/capabilities discovers units, limits and supported operations. Create through POST /diagrams with {name,type:process-simulator}. GET/PUT /diagrams/{diagramId}/simulation reads/replaces the complete semantic model at baseVersion; nodes, edges, particle-types, resources and scenarios have semantic CRUD under that same route. Shared resources, queues, complexity, routing, schedules, scaling, improvements and economic settings are explicit model fields, never inferred from canvas positions. POST /diagrams/{diagramId}/simulation/runs starts the same deterministic worker engine used by the UI with seed,durationSeconds,scenarioId,demandMultiplier,speed and animated options; it immediately returns a separately identifiable run. Poll GET .../runs/{runId}/state or /result. GET metrics, nodes/{nodeId}, resources/{resourceId}, bottlenecks and paged events inspect actual semantic state. POST pause,resume,stop,reset and seek control that run; exact POST simulation/compare compares runIds without mutating the models. Read only permits inspection and exact comparison; starting or controlling runs and model changes require Read + write. Headless/MAX execution needs no active canvas or animation loop, but the browser must remain connected for IndexedDB and local worker execution. Go and MCP have no separate simulation engine or shadow configuration. Live 2D scaling uses separate full native cards such as Counter 1/2/3: read-only anonymous capacity units with one shared logical model, actual aggregate occupancy and a shared queue. visualCapacity discovery exposes eight cards per bank and 256 additional cards globally, with explicitly labelled aggregate overflow. These cards are not separate persistent process nodes or named-worker business identities; 3D retains the logical model. Models, scenarios and bounded results remain browser-local; seeds and immutable input snapshots support deterministic replay and repeated demand stress tests."
